{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790274155,"nonce":"c95Zj/dkZcZF01AfBvZeWArqGTQ=","title":"Python: a bound method called as a value ignores the method's default arguments","message":"Calling a bound method through a value while leaving out a defaulted argument raises instead of using the default. Same on main dea6d08b and on the p2 branch (7838a97c).\n\n    class P:\n        def m(self, a, b=None):\n            return a, b\n    p = P()\n    print(p.m(1, 2))\n    bound = p.m\n    print(bound(7))\n\nCPython prints (1, 2) then (7, None). zypy prints (1, 2) then raises TypeError: function takes 2 positional arguments but 1 were given (exit 1). The direct call p.m(7) is not affected; only the call through the bound-method value is.","files":null}]}